        explanation:
          "Disability under Title II (SSDI) requires an inability to engage in any substantial gainful activity due to a medically determinable physical or mental impairment expected to last at least twelve months or result in death. SSA applies this through a mandatory five-step sequential evaluation: (1) is the claimant currently engaged in substantial gainful activity (if so, denied regardless of medical severity); (2) does the claimant have a severe impairment; (3) does the impairment meet or equal one of the Listing of Impairments, SSA's catalog of presumptively disabling conditions (if so, allowed automatically); (4) if not listed, can the claimant, given their Residual Functional Capacity (RFC), still perform their own past relevant work; and (5) if not, can they, given RFC plus age, education, and work experience, adjust to any other work existing in the national economy. Steps four and five are where most real disputes concentrate, since they require translating medical evidence into a specific functional capacity and then testing that capacity against actual vocational possibilities, often through a vocational expert's testimony at an ALJ hearing.",
