    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one framework, walking through a benefits dispute in the order it actually unfolds. The social-insurance-versus-welfare architecture is the lens every later concept gets read through, since it determines which eligibility test applies at all. Retirement, SSDI, SSI, unemployment, and workers' compensation each have their own substantive entitlement standard, but notice how often the same underlying question recurs in different clothing: did the claimant contribute enough, are they needy enough, or are they genuinely unable to work, each program picks one or more of those three questions and builds its own test around it. The claims pipeline, overpayment waiver, fraud-versus-error, and benefit coordination then apply across nearly all of these programs at once, which is exactly why they are covered last: they are the shared machinery, not a sixth substantive entitlement test. Miss the social-insurance-versus-welfare distinction at the start, and every later concept gets misapplied, a lawyer who brings SSI's resource-limit thinking to an SSDI case, or vice versa, is reasoning from the wrong program's rules entirely. And notice the asymmetric pairs running underneath the procedural half of this lecture: a default recovery rule paired with a fault-based waiver, a default fraud suspicion paired with an intent requirement that has to actually be proven, a combined-benefit windfall paired with a statutory offset that caps it. Learning this field well means knowing not just what each program promises, but exactly where the law pulls that promise back, and why.",
